# Lecture 11D

## From Distances to a Tree: Three- and Four-Sequence Examples

## Chapter 11

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 17, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Read one half of a symmetric distance matrix.
- Build a UPGMA tree from sequence distances.
- Calculate cluster distances, node heights, and branch lengths.
- Check fitted tree distances against the original matrix.

=== layout: section

# From Distances to a Tree

=== layout: definitions

# Distance Matrix

@body

- [*Sequence distance*]{underline=true}: a number summarizing differences between two sequences.
- [*Distance matrix*]{underline=true}: a table of the pairwise distances among sequences.
- [*Diagonal*]{underline=true}: entries comparing each sequence with itself. Values match on both sides.
- [*Cluster*]{underline=true}: a group of sequences joined during tree construction.

=== layout: one-panel

# We'll Ignore the Top Half to Avoid Confusion

@body

- The two halves of a distance matrix repeat the same pairwise distances.
- Use the lower half and skip the diagonal when finding the smallest value.
- A small distance means similar sequences, using the chosen distance measure.

=== layout: one-panel

# Building a Tree by UPGMA

@body

- Find the smallest distance and join those sequences or clusters.
- The new node height is half that distance, measured from the tips.
- Each new branch length = new height - child node height. Tips have height zero.
- For each new cluster distance, average all original pairs across the two groups.
- Repeat until one tree remains. UPGMA assumes a constant rate of change across lineages.

=== include: lect11c_rhino_steps.djot

=== layout: section

# Four Sequences: Work It Out

=== layout: one-panel

# Four Gene Sequences

@body

- a, b, c, and d label four gene sequences.
- The matrix entries are illustrative distances in arbitrary units, not years.
- A cluster label such as ac means the sequences a and c joined together.
- For each step, predict the next join and node height before looking at the answer.

=== include: lect11c_four_steps.djot


=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- A symmetric distance matrix lists each pair twice; read one half.
- UPGMA joins the closest clusters using average distances.
- A new node height is half the join distance; subtract child height for each branch.
- UPGMA assumes a constant rate across lineages. Its tree summarizes the original distances.

